{
  "$comment_purpose": "Correctness benchmark for the Language Understanding parser. Unlike datasets/language/prompts/examples.json (a few-shot/review library the prompt is designed and reviewed against), every input here is intentionally DISJOINT from every input in parser_prompt.txt, examples.json, negative_examples.json, and edge_cases.json -- an LLM scoring well here has not simply memorized an in-prompt example, it has generalized the parsing rules to unseen instructions.",
  "$comment_responsibility": "Measure parser correctness: given an input, does a candidate model's output match expected_output field-for-field (exact match for closed-vocabulary fields like goal/priority/constraint_type, semantic-equivalence for free-text fields like estimated_goal -- see $comment_scoring below)? This file holds ONLY realistic, answerable, non-ambiguous commands -- robustness and ambiguity-detection are measured by the sibling files failure_cases.json and ambiguity_cases.json respectively, deliberately kept separate so a single aggregate score can't hide a model that is strong on one dimension and weak on another.",
  "$comment_consumers": "Phase 3.6 (Testing & Benchmarking) is the primary consumer: it will run each 'input' through a candidate Language Agent + LLM pairing, validate the raw response against schemas.task.ParsedInstruction, and score it against 'expected_output'. Designed to be re-run unmodified against GPT, Qwen, Llama, Phi, or any future model -- nothing in this file's schema names a specific provider.",
  "$comment_scoring": "Recommended scoring approach for Phase 3.6: (1) schema validity -- does the raw output parse as a ParsedInstruction at all (pass/fail); (2) structural field match -- exact equality on task_type, goal, object, target, source_location, target_location, attributes, constraints, priority, needs_clarification (each scored independently, since a model can get most fields right and one wrong); (3) free-text field similarity -- estimated_goal and clarification_reason compared by semantic similarity or human judgment, never exact string match, since correct paraphrases are expected to differ across models.",
  "$schema_version": "1.0.0",
  "$prompt_version": "1.1.0",
